1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,386
A tailor measures in inches.

2
00:00:02,386 --> 00:00:05,185
A doctor writes your
height in centimetres.

3
00:00:05,185 --> 00:00:07,736
A lab weighs a medicine in milligrams.

4
00:00:08,136 --> 00:00:10,119
Different units, different tools.

5
00:00:10,119 --> 00:00:12,616
Yet every measurement
hides a little doubt.

6
00:00:13,066 --> 00:00:15,435
Aarav measures his bicycle wheel.

7
00:00:15,435 --> 00:00:18,030
One tape says sixty six centimetres.

8
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:20,916
A finer tape says sixty six point zero.

9
00:00:21,266 --> 00:00:22,166
Same wheel.

10
00:00:22,143 --> 00:00:25,976
So why does his teacher say the
second reading tells us more?

11
00:00:26,476 --> 00:00:29,830
By the end, you will read any
measurement digit by digit.

12
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:33,758
First, a measurement is a
number and a unit together.

13
00:00:34,108 --> 00:00:36,721
Without the unit, the
number means nothing.

14
00:00:36,721 --> 00:00:39,568
Five could be five metres,
or five millimetres.

15
00:00:39,968 --> 00:00:44,858
So in nineteen sixty, the world agreed
on one system: the International

16
00:00:44,858 --> 00:00:46,925
System of Units, or SI.

17
00:00:47,325 --> 00:00:49,029
SI has seven base units.

18
00:00:49,029 --> 00:00:53,318
The metre for length, the kilogram
for mass, and the second for time.

19
00:00:53,718 --> 00:00:57,288
The ampere for electric current,
and the kelvin for temperature.

20
00:00:57,688 --> 00:01:01,739
The mole for amount of substance, and
the candela for how bright a light

21
00:01:01,739 --> 00:01:02,639
is.

22
00:01:02,795 --> 00:01:06,187
Prefixes stretch a unit up
or down by powers of ten.

23
00:01:06,587 --> 00:01:08,104
Kilo means a thousand.

24
00:01:08,104 --> 00:01:12,571
Centi is a hundredth, milli a
thousandth, micro a millionth, and nano a

25
00:01:12,571 --> 00:01:13,471
billionth.

26
00:01:13,994 --> 00:01:16,922
Chemistry meets huge
numbers, and tiny ones.

27
00:01:17,272 --> 00:01:19,864
So we write them in scientific notation.

28
00:01:19,864 --> 00:01:23,777
A number from one to just under
ten, times a power of ten.

29
00:01:24,177 --> 00:01:26,823
Watch the decimal point on our bench.

30
00:01:26,823 --> 00:01:29,636
Take zero point zero zero zero one six.

31
00:01:30,036 --> 00:01:34,234
Hop the point one place right,
and the power drops to minus one.

32
00:01:34,234 --> 00:01:35,134
Again.

33
00:01:34,825 --> 00:01:37,306
Minus two, minus three, minus four.

34
00:01:37,706 --> 00:01:39,880
Now the number is one point six.

35
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:43,394
So we write one point six
times ten to the minus four.

36
00:01:43,794 --> 00:01:45,921
Big numbers go the other way.

37
00:01:45,921 --> 00:01:50,558
Two hundred thirty two point five
zero eight is two point three two five

38
00:01:50,558 --> 00:01:52,624
zero eight times ten squared.

39
00:01:53,274 --> 00:01:56,139
Each hop to the right
takes one from the power.

40
00:01:56,139 --> 00:01:57,661
Each hop left adds one.

41
00:01:58,061 --> 00:01:59,373
Pause and think.

42
00:01:59,373 --> 00:02:04,485
Is the power of ten for zero point
zero zero five positive, or negative?

43
00:02:07,685 --> 00:02:08,585
Negative!

44
00:02:08,498 --> 00:02:11,481
It is five times ten to the minus three.

45
00:02:11,481 --> 00:02:13,887
Small numbers get negative powers.

46
00:02:14,537 --> 00:02:16,648
How far can we trust a reading?

47
00:02:16,648 --> 00:02:21,099
Count its significant figures: the
digits we know for sure, plus one last

48
00:02:21,099 --> 00:02:22,212
uncertain digit.

49
00:02:22,562 --> 00:02:23,463
Rule one.

50
00:02:23,463 --> 00:02:25,605
Every non zero digit counts.

51
00:02:25,605 --> 00:02:28,814
Two eight five has three
significant figures.

52
00:02:29,564 --> 00:02:30,511
Rule two.

53
00:02:30,511 --> 00:02:33,295
Zeros between non zero digits count.

54
00:02:33,295 --> 00:02:36,033
Two point zero zero five has four.

55
00:02:36,783 --> 00:02:37,814
Rule three.

56
00:02:37,814 --> 00:02:42,082
Zeros before the first non
zero digit only hold the place.

57
00:02:42,082 --> 00:02:44,792
Zero point zero three has just one.

58
00:02:45,542 --> 00:02:46,459
Rule four.

59
00:02:46,459 --> 00:02:50,208
Zeros at the end count when
there is a decimal point.

60
00:02:50,208 --> 00:02:52,757
Zero point two zero zero has three.

61
00:02:53,507 --> 00:02:57,822
So Aarav's sixty six point zero
has three significant figures.

62
00:02:58,572 --> 00:02:59,576
Remember this.

63
00:02:59,576 --> 00:03:03,442
Every digit you write is a promise
about how well you measured.

64
00:03:04,142 --> 00:03:07,671
Now, a worked example,
using dimensional analysis.

65
00:03:07,971 --> 00:03:10,252
A jug holds two litres of milk.

66
00:03:10,252 --> 00:03:12,789
What is that volume in cubic metres?

67
00:03:13,289 --> 00:03:15,812
Write a unit factor that equals one.

68
00:03:15,812 --> 00:03:18,744
One litre is one thousand
cubic centimetres.

69
00:03:19,494 --> 00:03:23,650
Two litres, times one thousand
cubic centimetres per litre.

70
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:24,808
Litres cancel.

71
00:03:24,808 --> 00:03:27,016
Two thousand cubic centimetres.

72
00:03:27,766 --> 00:03:31,619
One cubic metre is ten to
the six cubic centimetres.

73
00:03:31,619 --> 00:03:36,622
So multiply by one cubic metre over
ten to the six cubic centimetres.

74
00:03:37,372 --> 00:03:39,097
Cubic centimetres cancel.

75
00:03:39,097 --> 00:03:43,172
The answer is two times ten to
the minus three cubic metres.

76
00:03:43,822 --> 00:03:46,980
Precision means your readings
agree with each other.

77
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:49,852
Accuracy means they agree
with the true value.

78
00:03:50,252 --> 00:03:53,211
The true mass is two
point zero zero grams.

79
00:03:53,211 --> 00:03:58,133
Student A gets one point nine five and
one point nine three: close together,

80
00:03:58,133 --> 00:03:59,482
but far from two.

81
00:03:59,482 --> 00:04:01,122
Precise, not accurate.

82
00:04:01,522 --> 00:04:05,694
Student B gets one point nine
four and two point zero five.

83
00:04:05,694 --> 00:04:07,758
Neither precise nor accurate.

84
00:04:08,158 --> 00:04:12,242
Student C gets two point zero
one and one point nine nine.

85
00:04:12,242 --> 00:04:13,804
Precise and accurate.

86
00:04:14,454 --> 00:04:15,919
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:16,269 --> 00:04:18,653
Some students count every zero.

88
00:04:18,653 --> 00:04:23,670
They say zero point zero zero five
zero has five significant figures.

89
00:04:24,070 --> 00:04:26,688
But leading zeros only hold the place.

90
00:04:26,688 --> 00:04:30,925
Only the five and the last zero
count: two significant figures.

91
00:04:31,575 --> 00:04:33,154
Where is this asked?

92
00:04:33,154 --> 00:04:37,710
As quick questions on significant
figures, scientific notation and unit

93
00:04:37,710 --> 00:04:38,622
conversion.

94
00:04:39,222 --> 00:04:40,526
Exam level now.

95
00:04:40,526 --> 00:04:44,515
A length is zero point zero
two zero five zero metres.

96
00:04:44,515 --> 00:04:49,842
How many significant figures, and how
is it written in scientific notation?

97
00:04:49,842 --> 00:04:51,201
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:56,601 --> 00:04:57,979
The answer is A.

99
00:04:57,979 --> 00:05:02,711
Four figures, two point zero five
zero times ten to the minus two.

100
00:05:03,211 --> 00:05:05,493
The two leading zeros do not count.

101
00:05:05,993 --> 00:05:10,369
The two, the zero after it, the
five, and the last zero all count.

102
00:05:10,369 --> 00:05:11,517
That makes four.

103
00:05:12,017 --> 00:05:15,820
The point hops two places right,
so the power is minus two.

104
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:19,093
B drops the last zero,
and D gets the sign wrong.

105
00:05:19,693 --> 00:05:21,158
Here is your recap.

106
00:05:21,158 --> 00:05:22,663
Screenshot this card.

107
00:05:25,863 --> 00:05:28,700
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

108
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:30,000
One.

109
00:05:29,704 --> 00:05:34,571
Write zero point zero zero zero
four two in scientific notation.

110
00:05:35,371 --> 00:05:36,271
Two.

111
00:05:35,845 --> 00:05:39,096
Can a set of readings be
precise, but not accurate?

112
00:05:39,896 --> 00:05:40,796
Three.

113
00:05:40,520 --> 00:05:45,106
Convert three inches to centimetres,
if one inch is two point five four

114
00:05:45,106 --> 00:05:46,038
centimetres.

115
00:05:46,938 --> 00:05:51,028
Next time: when a candle burns
away, where does its mass go?

116
00:05:51,028 --> 00:05:53,902
We find out in laws of
chemical combination.
